"""Board: hp-elitedesk-800-g3 — HP EliteDesk 800 G3 SFF (Skylake-S, Q270/vPro AMT).

Replaces //boards/cf-sz5 as the physical test machine (that unit was retired
2026-08-04). Identical hardware posture to //boards/hw-x64 (generic UEFI +
ACPI x86-64: GOP framebuffer, AHCI + NVMe, ACPI/PCI enumeration) — it shares
that board's driver list, bootfs staging map and feature tokens verbatim, the
same way //boards/cf-sz5 did. The ONE thing this board exists to change is the
serial console, and that difference is load-bearing for the same reason it was
on the CF-SZ5: this machine also has no COM1, so `kernel.serial=legacy` would
boot it stone silent.

PROVENANCE DISCIPLINE — read this before trusting any claim below. The CF-SZ5
board doc records a long, hard-won debugging history on THAT unit. This is a
DIFFERENT machine (different chipset generation, different firmware, different
NIC silicon revision). Nothing measured on the CF-SZ5 is assumed to hold here
just because the architecture rhymes. Every claim below is labelled:

  MEASURED — read directly off this unit, booted bare-metal into Alpine at L0,
    2026-08-04 from a complete hardware inventory capture.
  INHERITED, UNPROVEN — the CF-SZ5 hit this failure mode / needed this fix, and
    the same hardware shape is present here, but it has NOT been re-measured on
    this unit. Treat as a hypothesis the first real boot must check, not fact.
  UNKNOWN — not measured, not obviously inferable from the CF-SZ5 experience
    either.

== MEASURED on this unit ==

  Identity: HP EliteDesk 800 G3 SFF, board id 8299, Q270 chipset (Intel
  200-series PCH), Intel Core i5-6500 (Skylake-S, 4 cores / no HT, 3.2 GHz),
  12 GiB RAM (4 GiB + 8 GiB DDR4-2133).

  Serial console: Intel AMT Serial-over-LAN, PCI function 00:16.3
  [8086:a2bd], "200 Series/Z370 Chipset Family Keyboard and Text (KT)
  Redirection", class 0x070002 (a genuine 16550A, PIO not MMIO — same shape as
  the CF-SZ5's SOL function, different address). BAR0 is I/O 0x4088-0x408f;
  Linux enumerates it as `ttyS0 at I/O 0x4088 (irq = 19, base_baud = 115200)
  is a 16550A`. The matching cmdline is `ioport,<base>,<irq>`:
    * "ioport" is uart::ns8250::PioDriver's kConfigName (ns8250.h) — the
      config name for ZBI_KERNEL_DRIVER_I8250_PIO_UART.
    * the payload is zbi_dcfg_simple_pio_t, and its ParseConfig
      (//zircon/system/ulib/uart/parse.cc) reads exactly two integers in the
      order (base, irq); both accept 0x hex. This grammar and the ParseConfig
      behavior are generic kernel code, not CF-SZ5-specific, so they apply
      here unchanged.

  NIC: Intel I219-LM [8086:15e3] ("I219-LM5" per e1000.bind's device table
  comment) at 00:1f.6, Linux driver e1000e, MAC 3c:52:82:72:4a:fd. Already
  bind-listed: 0x15e3 appears in
  //src/cerise/drivers/e1000/meta/e1000.bind:41 and in e1000.cc's header
  comment, so no bind-list change was needed for this board (verified by
  reading those files, not assumed).

  Storage: AHCI at 00:17.0 with two SATA SSDs (/dev/sda INTEL 238.5G,
  /dev/sdb Apacer 111.8G) and NVMe at 01:00.0 (/dev/nvme0n1, PCI id
  1d97:1160 "Shenzhen Longsys... FORESEE P900", NVMe-identifies itself as
  "Lexar 256GB SSD" — a rebadged controller, not a discrepancy worth chasing).
  All three currently carry VMware VMFS partitions from a former life as an
  ESXi host; nothing on any disk needs preserving, and consuming the whole
  disk is authorised.

  iGPU: Skylake GT2 HD Graphics 530 at 00:02.0 — same GOP framebuffer path as
  hw-x64 and the CF-SZ5.

  USB: xHCI at 00:14.0.

  IRQ FIELD IS 0, NOT 19 — this reasoning is architecture-level, not
  CF-SZ5-specific, and was already generalized in-tree before this board was
  written: //zircon/kernel/arch/x86/ioapic.cc now passes GSIs >= 16 through
  instead of ASSERTing `isa_irq < NUM_ISA_IRQS`, so the historical CF-SZ5
  landmine (booting `ioport,<base>,19` outright panicking on that assert) is
  already closed generically. irq=0 is still the right choice regardless: it
  selects the kernel's SUPPORTED polling mode (platform/debug.cc's
  UartDriverHandoffLate treats a zero/absent IRQ as "no interrupt" and starts
  gUartPollTimer). uart::ns8250 transmits by polling the line-status
  register's tx_register_empty bit unconditionally (TxReady/Write), so
  console TX never depended on the interrupt in the first place; RX is
  drained by the poll timer. Moving to interrupt-driven RX would need GSI 19
  programmed into the IOAPIC with PCI trigger/polarity (level/low), which
  cannot be rehearsed under QEMU (ISA bus rejects irqnum >= 16 outright) and
  so would have to be proven on the real unit first — nobody has asked for
  that yet.

== INHERITED, UNPROVEN on this hardware ==

  * FIRMWARE HANDS THE OS 00:16.3 WITH I/O DECODE TURNED OFF. This was the
    CF-SZ5's actual root cause (COMMAND=0x0000 while BAR0 was programmed, so
    the port read back all-ones and every byte vanished into an unclaimed
    port). //zircon/kernel/phys/uart-console.cc's EnableIoDecodeForPort fix
    for this is BOARD-GENERIC — it scans for whichever type-0 PCI function
    owns the configured I/O BAR and turns its decode on, keyed off the port
    number, not off a device ID or a hardcoded address — so it should cover
    0x4088 unchanged with no code change required. Confirmed by reading the
    fix (it is genuinely general). But whether THIS unit's firmware exhibits
    the same decode-off condition at all has not been measured — the Alpine
    inventory capture ran Linux, whose PCI core enables device decode as an
    ordinary part of enumeration, so ttyS0 working under Linux does not tell
    us what Cerise's phys boot environment (which runs its own PCI probing
    before any generic OS PCI core touches the device) will see. This can
    only be checked by watching for uart-console.cc's fix path firing (or not
    being needed) on the first real boot.

  * AMT / e1000 COEXISTENCE. The CF-SZ5's e1000 driver history (see that
    board's docstring) found and fixed a real ordering bug in
    src/cerise/drivers/e1000/e1000.cc: e1000_init_hw_ich8lan zeroes RAR[1..11]
    and reprograms RAR[0], and reading the MAC AFTER that call (instead of
    before) let a since-fixed version of the driver invalidate the receive
    filter AMT's own traffic matches, taking AMT off the network entirely.
    That fix (read the MAC before InitHw, snapshot RAR[0], set/clear
    CTRL_EXT.DRV_LOAD around Stop/start) is GENERIC DRIVER CODE — it is not
    gated on board or device ID — so it applies to this NIC too without any
    change here. What is NOT re-proven: the CF-SZ5's six-boot AMT-health
    validation was against mac_type e1000_pch_spt (Sunrise Point / 100-series
    PCH, device id 0x156f). This board's I219-LM is device id 0x15e3 on a
    Q270 (200-series, "Union Point") PCH, which e1000_set_mac_type maps to a
    DIFFERENT mac_type (almost certainly e1000_pch_cnp, per
    e1000.cc:127-128's IsPchSptFamily / IsPchManageabilityFamily split — cnp
    is handled by the same code path, but has not been exercised against real
    ME firmware here). Whether AMT stays healthy through a Cerise boot on
    THIS silicon revision — ICMP reachable, :16992 web UI answering, SOL
    surviving the driver bind — is unmeasured and must be checked on first
    boot exactly as the CF-SZ5 was: verify AMT healthy before and after.

  * WHETHER FIRMWARE REDIRECTS POST/UEFI OUTPUT TO SOL. On the CF-SZ5 it does
    not — the pre-kernel shim's CERISE-SHIM-GOP-FB prints on the UEFI console
    only, and the first thing expected on SOL is the kernel's own
    "physload: Console configured as..." line. HP's firmware may behave
    differently (some HP business desktops do offer a "Redirection after BIOS
    POST" AMT option); not checked here. Its absence on first boot is not
    evidence of failure unless corroborated by the shim/kernel markers also
    being missing.

== UNKNOWN ==

  * Whether this Q270 board's GOP implementation has any quirks relative to
    the CF-SZ5's / hw-x64's (untested; assumed fine, standard OEM desktop
    firmware).
  * Whether the DRAM-less FORESEE/Lexar NVMe controller has any quirks under
    Cerise's nvme driver (untested).
  * Actual boot time / fallback-OS-return timing on this unit, i.e. this
    board's analogue of the CF-SZ5's "55s reboot baseline" discriminator for
    telling a hung boot from a running one. Not yet measured — establish it
    on first boot before relying on "does it come back" as a signal.

Because AMT SOL is a real UART the kernel drives directly (not a firmware
redirection service), the port should stay open for the whole boot — including
after ExitBootServices, where UEFI-console output would stop. That is a
property of the design, inherited from the CF-SZ5 case, not yet independently
re-confirmed on this unit.
"""

load("//assembly:defs.bzl", "cerise_board")
load(
    "//boards/hw-x64:drivers.bzl",
    "HW_X64_BOOTFS",
    "HW_X64_BOOT_DRIVERS",
    "HW_X64_PROVIDED_FEATURES",
)

package(default_visibility = ["//visibility:public"])

cerise_board(
    name = "hp-elitedesk-800-g3",
    platform = "//platforms:x64-fuchsia",
    # The hw-x64 boot-driver list, e1000 included -- this board's AMT Serial-
    # over-LAN and the e1000-driven NIC are the same PHY (I219-LM), so whatever
    # the NIC driver does to this device also affects the only remote console.
    # See the docstring's "AMT / e1000 COEXISTENCE" section: the ordering fix
    # that keeps AMT alive is generic driver code and already applies here,
    # but has not yet been independently measured on this silicon revision
    # (device id 0x15e3, presumed mac_type e1000_pch_cnp) the way it was on
    # the CF-SZ5's 0x156f/e1000_pch_spt part.
    boot_drivers = HW_X64_BOOT_DRIVERS,
    provided_features = HW_X64_PROVIDED_FEATURES,
    # AMT Serial-over-LAN 16550A at PIO 0x4088 (measured on the unit; Linux:
    # `ttyS0 at I/O 0x4088 (irq = 19, ...) is a 16550A`). IRQ field is 0 = the
    # kernel's polling mode; the real GSI is 19. See the docstring.
    #
    # WALLCLOCK: override the shared base default (kernel.wallclock=pit, in
    # build/kernel/BUILD.bazel `kernel_storage_zbi`) with `=auto` — a later
    # CMDLINE item wins. `=auto` (not `=tsc`) is deliberate: autodetect picks
    # invariant TSC on this unit's metal (the clock we need), and degrades to
    # HPET under this board's own QEMU gate, whose TCG CPU model offers no
    # invariant TSC. Forcing `=tsc` instead would panic that gate ("Could not
    # satisfy kernel.wallclock choice"). We override rather than dropping the
    # base pin, because the pin is load-bearing for every OTHER x86 product;
    # only THIS unit needs a different clock.
    #
    # Why PIT is wrong here, measured 2026-08-04: with 4 CPUs on the PIT
    # wallclock the per-CPU heartbeats gap to the 3000 ms lockup threshold
    # (2996-3012 ms against a 1000 ms period; the "heartbeats" that do land are
    # the lockup detector's own diagnostic IPIs waking the stalled cores), idle
    # APs sit in MWAIT C1 for 0.6-2 s at a stretch, and boot never produces
    # userspace. On the autodetected TSC the same image's gaps drop to
    # 505-1075 ms and every timer behaves. WHY PIT starves AP timers on this
    # firmware is still open; TSC is simply the correct clock for an
    # invariant-TSC part regardless.
    #
    # kernel.x86.disable_x2apic: with x2APIC mode enabled (the default on this
    # CPUID), even a timer-healthy 4-CPU boot stalls before userspace produces
    # output; in xAPIC mode the identical image reaches the fileserver's :50051
    # in ~70 s from reset. Mechanism not yet root-caused (suspicion: HP's DMAR
    # x2APIC-opt-out, which Zircon does not consult); revisit before any
    # feature that needs x2APIC (>254 APIC IDs, IR) — this box needs neither.
    # Both measured on the unit 2026-08-04, each knob isolated first, then the
    # pair confirmed end to end.
    #
    # TEMPORARY, but safe to pin: the real fix is teaching apic_local_init to
    # consult the DMAR x2APIC-opt-out flag instead of forcing xAPIC per-board.
    # Until then this pin is not a hazard — lapic.cc's demotion path is
    # SDM-correct (passes x2APIC->disabled->xAPIC rather than clearing EXTD in
    # place), so a board arriving in x2APIC mode is demoted cleanly, not wedged.
    kernel_cmdline = [
        "kernel.serial=ioport,0x4088,0",
        "kernel.wallclock=auto",
        "kernel.x86.disable_x2apic=true",
    ],
    bootfs = HW_X64_BOOTFS,
)
